        "888984ae5caf7782ea143c562fee0bf0cf38b3d1"
      ],
      "title": "DNP3: Retrieve timestamp with ENC_TIME_MSECS|ENC_LITTLE_ENDIAN",
      "message": "DNP3: Retrieve timestamp with ENC_TIME_MSECS|ENC_LITTLE_ENDIAN\n\nThe DNP3 timestamp is a little endian 48-bit count of miliseconds\nsince the Unix epoch, i.e. ENC_TIME_MSECS|ENC_LITTLE_ENDIAN.\nUse that encoding directly instead of reimplementing it. Get the\nreturned nstime_t because a string representation is appended to a\nparent item in most cases.\n",
      "author_name": "John Thacker",
      "author_email": "johnthacker@gmail.com",
      "authored_date": "2025-07-08T07:21:32.000-04:00",
      "committer_name": "Eric Wetzel",
      "committer_email": "thewetzel@gmail.com",
      "committed_date": "2025-07-20T13:22:59.000-04:00",
      "trailers": {},
      "extended_trailers": {},
      "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/commit/c3619ce9dade950c2787100c3e7d8dfaa0fccb27"
    },
    {
      "id": "888984ae5caf7782ea143c562fee0bf0cf38b3d1",
      "short_id": "888984ae",
      "created_at": "2025-07-20T13:22:47.000-04:00",
      "parent_ids": [
        "29e1d64d1e93b90b66c939a1a53f66aee1042e69"
      ],
      "title": "DNP3: Reassemble when transport sequence number is reset on FIR bit",
      "message": "DNP3: Reassemble when transport sequence number is reset on FIR bit\n\nIEEE Std 1815-2012 (DNP3) 8.2.1.4 Rules\n\nRule 4: A transport segment with the FIR bit set may have any sequence\nnumber from 0 to 63 without regard to prior history.\n\nRule 5 2) A received transport segment having the FIR bit set shall\ncause the entire, in-progress transport segment-series to be\ndiscarded...\n\nThe full Rule 5 explains that DNP3 is not supposed to handle reordered\ntransport segments at all. In practice, we can reassemble reordered\nsegments, but for implementations that take advantage of Rule 4 (likely\nresetting the sequence number to 0 for each segment with the FIR bit\nset, treating the sequence number as a fragment number), we cannot\nreassemble segments that are reordered across a FIR segment.\n\nThe alternative implementation, where the sequence number is a rolling.\n6 bit counter that increments for each transport segment regardless\nof whether it is part of a series, is more robust to re-ordering. We\ncould have a preference to be able to handle more out-of-order frames\nif it is known that the sequence number is such.\n\nFix #20336\n",
      "author_name": "John Thacker",
      "author_email": "johnthacker@gmail.com",
      "authored_date": "2025-01-14T09:08:00.000-05:00",
      "committer_name": "Eric Wetzel",
      "committer_email": "thewetzel@gmail.com",
      "committed_date": "2025-07-20T13:22:47.000-04:00",
      "trailers": {},
      "extended_trailers": {},
      "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/commit/888984ae5caf7782ea143c562fee0bf0cf38b3d1"
    },
    {
      "id": "29e1d64d1e93b90b66c939a1a53f66aee1042e69",
      "short_id": "29e1d64d",
